WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:11.200
Chimera is equipped with an array of structure&nbsp;
analysis resources that allow you to analyze the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:16.640
protein environment around a ligand, visualize&nbsp;
interactions within the protein, and more.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:23.360
Here we have the myoglobin structure 1mbo,&nbsp;
which contains a heme group as shown.

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:27.840
To analyze the protein environment&nbsp;
around this heme group,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:32.000
we start by selecting it under the&nbsp;
non-standard residues category.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:39.200
Now we can select all residues within the vicinity&nbsp;
of this heme group by going to Select by Zone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:46.000
In the Zone Parameters, we see that all atoms and&nbsp;
bonds within 5 ångströms of the current selection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:52.640
will be selected. You can of course adjust these&nbsp;
parameters as needed. To fully include residues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:59.280
that only fall partially into the 5 ångström&nbsp;
range, check this box here. Upon clicking OK,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:07.120
the desired vicinity is selected. We can now&nbsp;
color this region by element by going to Actions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:15.440
Color, and By Element. And once we clear the&nbsp;
selection, we can see the coloration clearly.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:21.920
We can also use Chimera's analysis resources&nbsp;
to measure the distance between two atoms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:27.360
such as the iron of heme and the coordinating&nbsp;
nitrogen of the proximal histidine residue.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:33.440
To do this, we start by selecting both&nbsp;
atoms using Control, Shift, and left click.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:42.480
Then under Tools, Structure Analysis, we&nbsp;
open up the Distances panel. When we click&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:48.640
Create, we see the distance displayed&nbsp;
in the panel and in the graphics window.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:54.880
In addition to measuring distances,&nbsp;
you can also visualize hydrogen bonding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.880 --> 00:02:01.360
interactions within the structure. To do&nbsp;
this, go to Tools, Structure Analysis, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:09.600
Find H-Bond. If we apply the default settings, all&nbsp;
the hydrogen bonds within the structure are found.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:17.680
To find all hydrogen bonds involving a specific&nbsp;
component, such as this serine hydroxyl, first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:25.600
select your component of choice using Control and&nbsp;
left click, and then run the hydrogen bond search&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:32.720
again. Make sure to turn on this setting here to&nbsp;
restrict your search to the selected component.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:40.080
Now only hydrogen bonds involving&nbsp;
the serine hydroxyl are shown.

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:53.600
Here we have the hemoglobin structure&nbsp;
4hhb. We can use Chimera to compare this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:58.080
structure with the related myoglobin&nbsp;
structure that we have been analyzing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:04.960
To import the myoglobin structure, we go&nbsp;
to File, Fetch by ID, and type in: 1mbo.

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:11.920
The structure then enters the graphics window,&nbsp;
but it doesn't seem to be aligning with any of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:18.320
the hemoglobin chains. To solve this problem,&nbsp;
we can go to Tools, Structure Comparison,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:24.720
and select Matchmaker. Then we choose our&nbsp;
reference structure — the hemoglobin — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:31.360
the structure to match — the myoglobin. When we&nbsp;
click Apply, we see the myoglobin aligning with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:38.880
chain D of the hemoglobin. With&nbsp;
hemoglobin chains A, B, and C deleted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:44.080
we can now get a better look at the overlay.&nbsp;
Although there are some positional differences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:49.840
such as the alpha helices, overall there is&nbsp;
much similarity between the two structures.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:55.600
And as we see here, the positions of the key&nbsp;
histidine residues around the heme group are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.600 --> 00:04:05.120
well-conserved between the two structures,&nbsp;
as is the position of the heme group itself.

